          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-a498e457b1ec\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The speaker directly avows love as part of a plea for Nikki not to end or exclude them from the relationship. The utterance strongly presents a loving state as true. Its placement within a request for another chance gives it weak commitment-invoking force, but it does not itself clearly promise or undertake anything. Nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge rather than an emotionally charged avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The reconciliation plea creates limited uncertainty about performative force: the declaration may help renew the relationship, but the explicit undertaking remains weak.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A speaker directly tells Nikki, “I love you,” during an appeal not to be shut out and to be given another chance.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration is embedded in a remorseful, persuasive appeal: the speaker acknowledges having been foolish, emphasizes need, asks Nikki not to shut them out, and requests another chance. This supports reading the words principally as an avowal offered to justify reconciliation. The passage does not establish whether the avowal is sincere, manipulative, or successful, and those contextual possibilities do not alter its dominant truth-conditional mode.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The unhedged first-person declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal of a loving emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I’ve been a fool. But please don’t shut me out. I love you. I need you. Just give me a chance.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding remorse and requests frame the declaration as part of a reconciliation plea, supplying weak performative or relationship-renewing pressure but no explicit commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration supplies no description such as blurting, speaking involuntarily, or hearing oneself say the words.\",\"supports\":\"There is no positive evidence for independent expressive/reflexive force; emotional urgency alone does not support E.\",\"confidence\":0.92}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E naturally represents the core utterance as a strong avowal with slight relationship-invoking force and no independently evidenced expressive/reflexive discharge. No residual core-force dimension requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
